# Ohm's law

> Voltage equals current times resistance. Knowing any two, I can find the third.

## Formulas

$$V = I \times R$$

| Symbol | Means | Unit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| V | voltage | volts (V) |
| I | current | amps (A) |
| R | resistance | ohms (Ω) |

Rearranged to find the resistance:

$$R = \frac{V}{I}$$

## In my words

- Ohm's law ties voltage, current and resistance together.
- I'll use $R = V / I$ to work out which [resistor](resistors.md) a circuit needs.

## Mistakes and corrections

- **Watch the units.** The formula needs current in **amps**, so milliamps must be converted first: 1 mA = 1/1000 A, so 20 mA = 0.020 A. Putting in 20 instead of 0.020 gives an answer 1000 times too small.

